  "observations": {
    "nyuv2_0001": [
      "R reduces broad wall error, and its final image has less dense error than its best on this particular sample. L/J/S show much darker error maps than B0, with similar recognizable room geometry. S is visually very close to its Lbest parent; no substantial additional structural improvement is visible.",
      "Boundary matches remain sparse: R F1 rises about0.101to0.111, L andS both remain0, J falls0.018to0. Lower dense error for L/J/S does not restore the missed cabinet and chair discontinuities. S AbsRel best0.0200/final0.0199 is nearly its Lbest0.0197."
    ],
    "nyuv2_0770": [
      "All adapted methods have more cabinet/stove error than B0. R worsens visibly at final, particularly broad cabinet and worktop regions (AbsRel0.0711to0.0938). L/J/S look similar across their endpoints; none overturns B0 being better in dense error for this scene.",
      "L/J/S recover short cyan upper-cabinet and counter segments compared with B0/R, while many hood and object boundaries remain red. S resembles Lbest: F1about0.314best/0.315final versus parent0.313 andAbsRel0.0528/0.0527versusparent0.0536. These are small differences without a clearly visible new structural gain."
    ],
    "nyuv2_1449": [
      "All adapted predictions retain chair-back geometry and reduce the foreground-chair dense error relative to B0, but background/wall error remains. Some R corrections introduce spatially varying regions; its final AbsRel worsens0.0815to0.0866 while still belowB0.",
      "S andLbest are visually almost indistinguishable in depth and matched chair outlines. S AbsRel changes0.0878to0.0857 versus Lbest0.0882, while F1remainsabout0.644 and belowB0about0.662. R retainsF1about0.669/0.668; L/J have slightlylowerF1. Thin chair legs are still mainly missed in allmethods. No blanket edge-restoration claim is supported."
    ]
  },
